<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Are Sweeping the Floors.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Explain simple written instructions to peers. Communication: Facilitate discussions on classroom topics among peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain written instructions with simple steps and gestures (e.g., "Please, wipe down the desks." and mimics wiping down the desks). Can facilitate discussions by asking classmates questions, encouraging answers, and using gestures like nodding to prompt responses (e.g., "How can you help keep the classroom clean?"). Can summarize classroom responsibilities for peers, using basic words and gestures (e.g., "We put away our supplies." and points to a labeled supply bin).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of different cleaning actions (sweep, wipe, arrange, put away). Students shout out the action. Teacher asks, "What are we doing today?" and elicits "cleaning!" or "taking care of our class!" Then, the teacher shows a picture of a messy classroom and asks, "How can we make it clean?" activating prior vocabulary and connecting to the theme "We Are Sweeping the Floors."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple written instruction card (e.g., "Please, wipe the desks.") and explicitly models how to explain it to a peer using clear words and gestures. "Look! This says, 'Please, wipe the desks.' So, I will wipe the desks, like this." (Teacher mimics wiping motion). Then, "Can you wipe the desks, please?" The teacher also models asking a facilitating question: "How can you help keep the classroom clean?" while nodding.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I only reading the words, or am I showing with my hands too?" (Showing with hands, explaining).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I ask my friend, 'How can you help?'" (Yes).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple "Classroom Cleaning Plan" chart or list on the whiteboard or projected. It assigns simple tasks using imperatives and present continuous, e.g., "Group 1: Sweep the floor. (We are sweeping the floors.)", "Group 2: Wipe the tables. (We are wiping the tables.)", "Group 3: Arrange the chairs. (We are arranging the chairs.)" The teacher highlights target vocabulary (sweep, wipe, arrange, chair, table, floor) and grammar (present continuous, imperatives).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will explain simple written instructions to peers and facilitate discussions on classroom topics among peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a task on the plan: "What is Group 1 doing?" (They are sweeping the floors.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who needs to wipe the tables?" (Group 2.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of someone arranging chairs: "Which group is this?" (Group 3. They are arranging the chairs.)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining instructions and facilitating discussion using Present continuous ("We are sweeping."), Imperatives ("Sweep the floor."), Modal "can" ("Can you help?"), polite forms ("Please, sweep."), and vocabulary (chair, desk, mop, broom, sweep, wipe, arrange, tidy).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Match and Explain:</b> Students receive cards with simple written instructions (e.g., "Put away the books carefully," "Clean the whiteboard quickly") and picture cards showing these actions. In pairs, students match the instruction to the picture. Then, one student explains the instruction to their partner using words and gestures, like a mediator. E.g., "This card says 'Put away the books carefully.' So, we are putting away the books, like this." (Teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of /s/ in 'supplies' and /r/ in 'arrange').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Frames:</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on the board for explaining and facilitating: "Group [Number], you are [verb + -ing] the [noun]." "Please, [imperative verb] the [noun]." "Can you help [verb] the [noun]?" "How can you help keep our classroom clean?" Students work in groups of three. One student acts as the "explainer," using the frames and gestures to explain tasks from the sample cleaning plan to their peers. Another peer asks a clarifying question or offers help ("Can I help you sweep?"), and the third peer responds or confirms understanding.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain a Task:</b> The teacher gives each pair a different specific cleaning task written on a slip of paper (e.g., "Tidy the supply cabinet," "Wipe the windows," "Organize the desks"). Each student takes turns explaining their assigned task to their partner, using gestures and including "please" and "can" where appropriate. The partner must listen and then explain it back to show understanding. Emphasis is on clear explanation and correct use of target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
            </ol>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> Students work in their established groups to finalize their "Classroom Clean-Up Plan" (21st Century Skills Project), which they started in previous lessons. For this Mediation lesson, the task is for each group to explain *their* written plan to another group or to the whole class. They must clearly explain roles and responsibilities, using imperatives and present continuous. They are encouraged to use gestures to clarify actions and to facilitate discussion by asking questions like "How can you help keep our classroom tidy?" or "What are we doing next week?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Format:</b> Small group presentations/explanations. Each group has a turn to explain their 'Classroom Clean-Up Plan' using their written plan as a reference. The explaining group should make sure to include statements like, "We are sweeping the floors on Mondays. Please, sweep quickly!" or "Sara, you are wiping the desks. Can you help wipe down the desks?" They must use polite language and actively engage the 'audience' group in the discussion.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on clarity of explanation, use of gestures, and active facilitation of discussion. Peers can use a simple "Thumbs Up/Thumbs Down" for understanding, or a short checklist for "Clear explanation?" and "Used gestures?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, as part of their group's presentation, must: 1) Explain at least two specific cleaning instructions from their Classroom Clean-Up Plan to their peers, using simple words and gestures. 2) Ask at least one question to another peer to facilitate discussion about classroom cleaning responsibilities (e.g., "How can you help clean our classroom?"). 3) Summarize one responsibility using basic words and gestures (e.g., "We put away our supplies." while pointing).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially /r/ in "arrange" and /s/ in "supply".</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear explanation of instructions with appropriate gestures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (sweep, wipe, arrange, put away, clean, tidy, desk, floor, trash can).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of present continuous ("We are sweeping.") and imperatives ("Please, wipe.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of "please" and "can" for politeness and offering help.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask a peer a question to facilitate discussion.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I explained how to ________ and I used my hands to show ________."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One question I asked my friends was: ________."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining the cleaning plan to my classmates?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I do well when explaining and asking questions today?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., student engagement with gestures, use of "please" for politeness, students asking questions).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to better explain instructions or facilitate discussions in the future? (e.g., more practice with compound words like "whiteboard" for clarity, a visual aid for question prompts).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Taking Care of Our Class" theme and its 21st Century Skills project. The next unit will introduce new vocabulary and a new scenario, but the skills of explaining, collaborating, and facilitating discussions will continue to be reinforced in future communication tasks.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>